CHMOD(1)

NAME
     chmod - change who may read, write or run a file

SYNOPSIS
     chmod mode file ...

DESCRIPTION
     Every file has three sets of permissions: for its owner (u), for
     its group (g) and for everyone else (o). Each set can allow
     reading (r), writing (w) and running (x). ls -l shows them.

     The mode says what to change: who, then + to add or - to take
     away, then which permission.

         chmod go-w notes       group and others may no longer write
         chmod u+x script       the owner may now run it

     A mode may also be given as a number, such as 644 or 755.

     The s permission is special. On a program, u+s (setuid) makes the
     program run with the authority of the owner of the file, whoever
     starts it. u-s takes that away. In ls -l it shows as an s where
     the x of the owner would be.

     Only the owner of a file, or root, may change its mode.

SEE ALSO
     ls(1)
